<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · mountain</p><h1 class="enc-title">Great St Bernard Pass</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Great Saint Bernard Pass</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Great St Bernard Pass is an Alpine pass at about 2,470 metres linking the Swiss canton of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valais">Valais</a> with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aosta">Aosta</a> valley in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. Used since Roman times, it carried traffic between northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and Italy throughout the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>. A hospice founded there in the 11th century by Bernard of Menthon gave the pass its name and later became known for its rescue dogs; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> crossed it with an army in 1800.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/great-st-bernard-pass.jpg" alt="Great St Bernard Pass" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Hagai Agmon-Snir حچاي اچمون-سنير חגי אגמון-שניר · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:GreatStBernardPassAugust232023_03.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
